];

const faq = [
  { q: 'Do ravens live in groups?', a: 'Yes — non-breeding ravens (juveniles and unpaired adults) typically live in groups, gathering at communal roosts that may contain dozens to hundreds of individuals. These roosts are not random aggregations but information-transfer networks, where naive birds follow successful foragers to food sources. Even paired, territorial adult ravens associate with each other and are social within their territory. The idea that ravens are solitary birds describes only the territorial aspect of established breeding pairs, which defend a home range — but this is a small part of the raven life cycle.' },
  { q: 'Are ravens more social than crows?', a: 'Ravens and crows (American Crow, Carrion Crow) are both highly social corvids, but their social organisations differ. Crows form stable family groups that often help raise subsequent broods (cooperative breeding). Ravens form pairs that maintain territories but do not typically show cooperative breeding; instead, non-breeding ravens form distinct "floater" flocks that operate outside the territorial adult population. Both species use communal roosts. Crows may be somewhat more family-socially oriented; ravens more socially complex at the non-breeding group level.' },
  { q: 'Why do people think ravens are solitary?', a: 'Several factors contribute to the solitary raven image: literary tradition (Poe\'s raven, the lone raven of Gothic imagery) portrays a single, brooding bird; territorial breeding pairs are often seen alone on their territory without obvious companions; and ravens in myth (Odin\'s Huginn and Muninn; Apollo\'s messenger) tend to appear as paired or solo emissaries rather than in flocks. The cultural image has been projected onto the bird\'s biology, obscuring the genuinely complex, group-oriented social life of non-breeding ravens.' },
];
