    <h1>Myth: Ravens Are Solitary Birds</h1>
  </header>

  <section class="qa-snippet" aria-label="Direct answer">
    <p class="verdict verdict-false"><strong>Verdict: False</strong></p>
    <p><strong class="claim-science">[science]</strong> Ravens are not solitary birds. Non-breeding ravens form large communal roosts and foraging groups; territorial breeding pairs maintain close, year-round partnerships. Raven social life is complex, with separate "floater" and "territorial" societies, information networks at roosts, and sophisticated social relationships. The solitary raven image derives from literary tradition and the territorial behaviour of established pairs — a small part of raven biology.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="qa-section">
    <h2>Where the Myth Comes From</h2>
    <p>The solitary raven is a powerful cultural image: Poe's brooding black bird perched alone above a door; Odin's messengers flying singly across the nine worlds; the raven on a Celtic battlefield standing apart. These images emphasise the raven's intelligence, mystery, and ominous solitude.</p>
    <p>In nature, territorial breeding ravens are often observed alone or in pairs on their home range — if you live near a raven territory, you will typically see only one or two birds there. This territorial pair-solitude is real but represents only a subset of raven social life, and only during the breeding season.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="qa-section">
    <h2>The Two Raven Societies</h2>
    <p><strong class="claim-science">[science]</strong> Common raven populations are divided into two distinct social groups that operate in different spaces and by different social rules:<sup><a href="#src-1">1</a></sup></p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Territorial adults:</strong> established breeding pairs that hold and defend a home range year-round. They are dominant at food within their territory. These birds appear "solitary" because they exclude other ravens from their space.</li>
      <li><strong>Non-breeding floaters:</strong> juveniles (1–3+ years old before first breeding), failed breeders, and unpaired adults that move as a group across the landscape. They use communal roosts — sometimes containing hundreds of birds — as information hubs, following successful foragers to food the next morning.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>The two societies interact at food: floater groups can overwhelm territorial pairs at large food patches (carcasses, dumps) through sheer numbers. This creates the paradox of a "solitary" territorial bird that is routinely invaded by crowds of its own species.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="qa-section">
    <h2>Communal Roosts: The Evidence</h2>
    <p><strong class="claim-science">[science]</strong> Communal raven roosts — concentrated aggregations of birds sleeping together at a site — are well-documented across the raven's range. Key facts:<sup><a href="#src-1">1</a></sup><sup><a href="#src-2">2</a></sup></p>
    <ul>
      <li>Winter roosts in New England (Heinrich's study area) regularly contained 50–200+ ravens at single sites.</li>
      <li>Roost sites are used year after year — birds return to the same locations across seasons.</li>
      <li>Naive birds at roosts follow successful foragers to food sources the next day — the roost functions as an information centre.</li>
      <li>The social behaviour at roosts — play, vocalisations, dominance interactions — is complex and ongoing, not merely passive sleeping aggregation.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section class="qa-section">
    <h2>Social Behaviour Within Pairs</h2>
    <p><strong class="claim-science">[science]</strong> Even the territorial breeding pair — the most "solitary" unit of raven social life — is profoundly social within itself. Mated ravens:<sup><a href="#src-1">1</a></sup></p>
    <ul>
      <li>Maintain close proximity year-round, not only during breeding season.</li>
      <li>Engage in allopreening (mutual feather care), billing, and vocalisation duets.</li>
      <li>Coordinate foraging strategies and territory defence.</li>
      <li>Display loyalty through years-long pair bonds that may last a lifetime.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>This pair sociality is qualitatively different from lone existence — a territorial raven pair is a close alliance, not two solitary individuals sharing space.</p>
  </section>

